WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.000
The ancient city of Petra, carved into rose-red sandstone cliffs, was established around 300 BCE.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.500
It served as the capital of the Nabataean Kingdom, a wealthy trading civilization in modern-day Jordan.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.000
The city's most famous structure, Al-Khazneh, was originally built as a mausoleum for a Nabataean king.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.500
Visitors approach Petra through a narrow gorge called the Siq, flanked by towering cliffs on both sides.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.000
The Nabataeans were masters of water management, constructing an elaborate system of dams and cisterns.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.500
These engineering feats allowed Petra to thrive in the harsh desert environment for centuries.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000
After the Roman annexation in 106 CE, the city gradually declined and was eventually abandoned.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.500
It remained unknown to the Western world until Swiss explorer Johann Ludwig Burckhardt rediscovered it in 1812.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Today, Petra is recognized as a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the New Seven Wonders of the World.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.000
Visitors from around the globe come to marvel at this extraordinary testament to human ingenuity.

